---

## 5) “No Surprise Orders” Test Rule

Any change that can alter trading behavior MUST include at least one of:
- unit test for pure logic
- integration test for pipeline wiring
- replay/golden test proving no behavioral drift

If execution is enabled in any environment:
- there must be a kill-switch test
- there must be a data-stale circuit breaker test

---

## 6) What to Assert (Institutional Style)

Prefer asserting on:
- emitted events (facts)
- resulting projections (orders/positions/pnl)
instead of asserting on internal function call order.

Events are the audit truth.

---

## 7) Property-Based Tests (Recommended for Trading Core)

High value targets:
- OMS FSM transitions:
  - random valid sequences must never violate invariants
  - duplicates must not change final state
- risk policies:
  - projected exposure must never exceed max if allowed
- normalization:
  - must not crash on malformed messages; must fail fast cleanly

---

## 8) CI Gate (Required Expectations)

Minimum gates for merging:
- ruff/format/lint pass
- mypy/pyright (at least for core models/contracts)
- unit tests pass
- integration tests pass
- replay tests pass (if touched core flows)

A PR that changes OMS/risk/execution without tests is invalid.

---

## 9) Change Control Principle

If you can’t express the behavior change as:
- “event sequence before vs after”
then you don’t understand the change enough to ship it.

Testing is the proof of understanding.
